23:58:54 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I can totally understand why you'd feel that way—it's a really natural conclusion to draw based on what you're seeing. The thing is, what you're describing is a powerful example of how our perception can be shaped by a biased sample. News outlets and viral stories have a strong negativity bias: a single horrific incident caught on video will be broadcast globally 24/7, because it's shocking and important. Meanwhile, the millions of perfectly ordinary, kind, or unremarkable interactions that happened that same day between people of different races—on subways, in shops, at work, between neighbours—will never make the news because "People Were Polite to Each Other Today" isn't a headline. So, what you're hearing is a heavily concentrated version of the worst the country has to offer. It's a bit like judging an entire ocean by only examining a few teaspoons of water scooped from the most polluted spot. That's not to dismiss the stories you're hearing. Those incidents are real, terrifying, and often symptomatic of the deep systemic issues we just talked about. But they represent the actions of a vocal, dangerous minority and the failures of certain systems, not the constant, everyday reality for the whole population of 330 million diverse people. If someone's entire "experience" of the U.S. came exclusively from that stream of worst-case scenarios, they'd be missing the vast, boring, and mostly decent reality in between. Does that distinction between the concentrated news feed and the sprawling, complicated reality make sense?
